# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository and checked out with the
# platform's own convention, so the content tree is CRLF on Windows and LF
# elsewhere. Nothing that reads a file may depend on which one it gets.
#
# This is not theoretical. An LF-only pattern does not fail loudly against a CRLF
# checkout — it matches nothing, and every field it would have captured reads as
# absent. Three copies of one frontmatter regex silently cost the search index
# 346 of 348 page titles and disabled every `id:` cross-page link alias. Parse
# page text through `FRONTMATTER_RE` / `splitLines` in lib/docs-model/resolve.ts,
# which accept both.
* text=auto

# npm always writes this file with LF; letting it check out as CRLF means the
# next `npm install` rewrites every line and the diff is pure noise.
package-lock.json text eol=lf

*.png binary
